\caption{Decisions from reported scores. (a) Synthetic worlds in which all $128$ configurations are equally accurate: rate of declaring that tuning helped over the default configuration (one-sided $5\%$ rules; $B$ configurations evaluated; intervals over $6$ worlds $\times500$ draws). (b) Real pool: the same rate in the draws in which the tuned configuration is not better than the default on the items outside $S$, for all $128$ configurations and for the $74$ configurations within $0.02$ of each other in accuracy (a near-null, not an exact null); $B=100$, intervals from $40$ resampled pools and therefore coarse. (c) Two independent searches, $A$ with $100$ and $B$ with $20$ (5:1) or both $20$ (1:1) evaluations: fraction of draws in which the reported score favours $A$.}
\label{fig:decisions}
\end{figure}

\emph{Tuned versus untuned.}
On synthetic worlds in which all configurations are exactly equally accurate and $n=120$, the naive test declares that tuning helped in $32\%$ of draws when $20$ configurations are evaluated and $54\%$ when $100$ are, against a nominal $5\%$; the max-t test declares in $1.6\%$ and $4.5\%$ and the held-out test in $6\%$ (Figure~\ref{fig:decisions}).
On the real pool tuning does help on average (mean fresh gain $+0.019$), so the naive test declares a help in $68\%$ of draws at $n=120$ with $100$ evaluations; what it overstates is the size.
Among the draws in which the selected configuration is not better than the default on fresh items it declares a help in $4.5\%$ (interval $0$--$0.21$), max-t in $1.1\%$.
The comparison against the densest window of $74$ configurations within $0.02$ in accuracy is a near-null, not an exact null, and no \emph{above nominal} judgement is made from it: there the naive test declares a help in $9.7\%$ of the draws in which the true gain is not positive, against $1.4\%$ for max-t.

\emph{Tuned versus tuned.}
When system~A searches $100$ configurations and system~B $20$, the larger search has the higher reported score in $74\%$ of draws on the synthetic exact null and in $57\%$ (own search sets) to $71\%$ (shared set) on the real pool, although the true difference of the deployed configurations is zero ($0.0005$ on the real pool); at equal budgets it is $45\%$ and $47\%$.
The significance test rarely fires ($2\%$ synthetic, $2$--$5\%$ real), so the distortion is in the direction of the reported comparison, not in the size of the test.

